  "result": "{\"seen_before\": true, \"matches\": [\"WSL#4851\"], \"answer\": \"This looks like WSL#4851, 'Localhost relay does not support dual-mode sockets'. `ss` shows k3s listening on `*:6443`, which is a dual-stack IPv6 socket. In NAT mode the Windows-to-WSL localhost relay forwards that port on `::1`, so `localhost` works. It does not forward it on IPv4 `127.0.0.1`. The ticket describes the same split: `::1` connects and `127.0.0.1` is refused. The archived ticket does not show a fix. It gives these workarounds:\\n\\n1. Make the service listen on a plain IPv4 address, such as `0.0.0.0` or `127.0.0.1`, so the relay forwards it on IPv4. For k3s, try setting `bind-address: 0.0.0.0` in `/etc/rancher/k3s/config.yaml`. That k3s option is my suggestion and is not in the archived ticket.\\n2. Point clients on Windows at `localhost` or `[::1]` instead of `127.0.0.1`. Headlamp does this if its kubeconfig server URL is `https://localhost:6443`.\\n3. In a hosts file entry, use `::1` in place of `127.0.0.1`.\\n\\nThe archive also has WSL#14154, a dual-stack IPv4 refusal inside WSL itself. It is not the same problem as yours, because yours is Windows to WSL.\"}",
